@flexiarg problems/per-tick-mismatch-instruments-outer-loop-gain
@title Per-Tick Mismatch Instruments Outer-Loop Gain
@style pattern
@why war-room/wr-27-a-loop-is-born-instrumented-for-its-gain
@holds-at R8

! conclusion: Use per-tick mismatch as the gain reading for the mission-conditional-reward outer loop and surface whether returned reward changes its next action.

  + context: Red ring at R8, ruling WR-27, established 2026-08-22 in
    p4ng/empirics-futon/wr-overlay.edn. Supplied by M-mission-conditional-reward,
    which owns the reward path whose outer loop should consume the mismatch.
    + salience: 香, by duration. The outer loop has been dead since 2026-07-14.
      Persistence is itself the perception — the futonic-logic instance of 香 is
      "this gap-PSR has appeared 3 times", a recurrence noticed before it is
      named — and a loop that has not updated in six weeks is that signal at
      its plainest. Nothing external reports it; it is visible only to whoever
      reads the per-tick mismatch, which is the defect restating itself.
    + whose problem: the apparatus's, and latently the operator's, since a
      learning loop that is not learning spends his time without repaying it.

  + IF:
    The reward path produces a per-tick mismatch that can measure what the outer loop should change next.

  + HOWEVER:
    The per-tick mismatch is the gain reading, and the outer loop has been dead since 2026-07-14.

  + THEN:
    Record the mismatch at every tick, route it into the outer-loop update, and report whether it changes the subsequent reward action.

  + BECAUSE:
    A loop is instrumented for its gain only when the returned mismatch is both observable and connected to what the loop does next.
    ?evidence(required): No source row demonstrates a live outer-loop update after 2026-07-14 driven by the per-tick mismatch.
